Most digital transformation programs fail not because systems are missing, but because authority over product data is fragmented across them.

A true digital thread is not integration. It is a governed, change-controlled lineage of information across the product lifecycle.

PLM defines engineering intent. ERP controls material and commercial execution. MES records production reality. QMS captures deviations and compliance evidence. ALM governs software and system behavior.

The problem appears at the boundaries: engineering revisions diverge from manufacturing execution, quality records lose configuration context, and traceability breaks despite functioning integrations.

This article proposes an architectural approach based on:

authoritative system ownership;
governed handoffs between lifecycle stages;
revision and change propagation rules;
canonical object relationships;
end-to-end traceability across engineering and operations.

The objective is not moving data faster. It is preserving product truth from design through production and compliance.
